nodecode-elevenlabs-music is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins
  commit 1e326ddd28ae261821d7714d106fe114999a0bba
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's agent half in Common Lisp: its eight tools
(elevenlabs_music_compose, _compose_plan, _draft_plan, _upload, _stems,
_from_video, _finetunes, _job) with their arguments and limits, the
ElevenLabs endpoints they call -- /v1/music/detailed, /v1/music/plan,
/v1/music/finetunes, /v1/music/upload, /v1/music/stem-separation and
/v1/music/video-to-music -- the xi-api-key header, the multipart and
multipart/mixed answers, the ZIP the stem endpoint returns, the output_format
and model choices, the local file checks, the per-track JSONL library, and
the background job a long generation runs in. Its hosting surface is
Nodecode's: the plugin's config_schema is the cell's section, and the tools
are registered with NODE:REGISTER-TOOL instead of a Hermes tool registry.

Left out, because it is Hermes's own surface and not the plugin's work: the
Hermes Desktop music card and music library tab (desktop/), their ffmpeg
WAV/FLAC downloads, and the chat-app instruction that arms a background
sleep wake so a long song is delivered later. The plugin's bundled
`elevenlabs-music` skill is not shipped as a Nodecode skill either; the same
guidance is the cell's (help :elevenlabs-music) primer. Job records live in
the process rather than for seven days on disk, and a track's real length is
not parsed out of the audio frames. No line of the original Python, its
Desktop bundle or its tests is carried over.
